        <div class="post-meta">
          <span>Updated July 2026</span>
          <span>•</span>
          <span>EV Route Guide</span>
        </div>
      </header>

      <div class="post-content">
        <p>
          Range remains the most discussed specification when shoppers consider an electric vehicle. While the average American drives fewer than 40 miles per day, the psychological comfort of a large battery buffer matters for road trips, unexpected detours, and cold weather. In 2026, several EVs offer EPA-rated ranges exceeding 350 miles, with a few pushing past 400 miles under ideal conditions.
        </p>

        <h2>Top 8 Longest-Range EVs in the US</h2>
        <p>
          The following eight electric vehicles represent the best options for buyers who prioritize minimizing charging stops. Rankings are based on EPA combined range estimates for the longest-range trim available in the United States.
        </p>

        <h3>1. Lucid Air Dream Range</h3>
        <p>
          The Lucid Air Dream holds the crown for maximum EPA range at 516 miles. Built on a highly efficient 900-volt architecture with a 118 kWh battery pack, the Air achieves over 4.5 miles per kWh despite its luxury sedan size and powerful dual-motor drivetrain. Real-world highway cruising typically yields 420 to 480 miles depending on speed. The tradeoff is price: the Dream starts well above six figures, placing it firmly in the luxury category.
        </p>

        <h3>2. Mercedes EQS 450+</h3>
        <p>
          Mercedes' flagship electric sedan delivers an EPA-rated 350 miles in the rear-wheel-drive 450+ configuration, though some real-world tests have exceeded 400 miles in warm weather at moderate speeds. The 108.4 kWh battery and slippery 0.20 drag coefficient contribute to its efficiency. The EQS prioritizes ride comfort and interior silence over sporty handling, making it an exceptional long-distance cruiser.
        </p>

        <h3>3. Tesla Model S Long Range</h3>
        <p>
          The Tesla Model S Long Range is EPA-rated at 405 miles. Tesla's continued refinements to motor efficiency and battery chemistry keep the Model S competitive despite its age. Supercharger access remains a significant advantage for road trips, with thousands of reliable 250-kW stalls across the country. Real-world range on highways at 70 mph typically falls between 320 and 370 miles, depending on weather and elevation changes.
        </p>

        <h3>4. BMW iX xDrive50</h3>
        <p>
          BMW's electric flagship SUV offers an EPA-rated 307 miles from its 111.5 kWh usable battery. While the number is lower than some sedans, the iX is a large, heavy vehicle with standard all-wheel drive. Its efficiency is impressive for the segment, and independent range tests have shown it can exceed EPA estimates in mixed driving. The iX supports fast charging up to 195 kW, allowing practical road trip stops.
        </p>

        <h3>5. Hyundai Ioniq 6 Long Range</h3>
        <p>
          The Hyundai Ioniq 6 is the efficiency champion of the mainstream market, with a rear-wheel-drive Long Range trim EPA-rated at 361 miles. Its teardrop shape and 800-volt architecture enable both low energy consumption and ultra-fast charging. Real-world reports consistently show the Ioniq 6 delivering at or above its EPA rating in warm weather, a rarity among EVs. For buyers who want maximum range without a luxury price tag, the Ioniq 6 is compelling.
        </p>

        <h3>6. Tesla Model 3 Long Range</h3>
        <p>
          The updated Tesla Model 3 Long Range returns an EPA estimate of 341 miles. It remains one of the most efficient EVs on sale, thanks to its compact footprint, lightweight design, and refined motor controls. Access to the Supercharger network and strong residual values make it a safe choice for buyers who need range on a budget. Highway cruising at 75 mph typically yields 280 to 310 miles before needing a charge.
        </p>

        <h3>7. Tesla Model Y Long Range</h3>
        <p>
          The Tesla Model Y Long Range offers an EPA-rated 310 miles. As a compact SUV, it sacrifices some efficiency compared to the Model 3 but adds cargo capacity and a higher seating position. For families who need space but still want strong range and access to the best charging network, the Model Y is the default recommendation. Real-world highway range is usually 260 to 290 miles in moderate weather.
        </p>

        <h3>8. Rivian R1S Dual Motor Max</h3>
        <p>
          Rivian's R1S Max Pack provides an EPA-rated 390 miles, an extraordinary figure for a full-size, three-row, all-electric SUV designed for off-roading. The large 141 kWh battery pack adds weight and cost, but it enables genuine adventure travel without range anxiety. Real-world range depends heavily on driving style and terrain, with highway cruising typically returning 300 to 340 miles.
        </p>

